# Fathom — Scoring, lives, depth, and audio

This file fixes what a dive is worth, what a catch costs, how a cleared maze
descends to a deeper one, what depth scales, and the audio the game plays. The
maze is in `specs/maze.md`, the plankton and the drifters in
`specs/gameplay.md`, the fog, brightness and sonar in `specs/sensing.md`, the
controls in `specs/movement.md`, the predators in `specs/predators.md`, and the
screens the values below are shown on in `specs/ui.md`.

## Scoring

| Event | Points |
| --- | --- |
| The forager eats a plankton | `SCORE_PLANKTON` (`10`) |
| The forager eats a bonus drifter | `SCORE_DRIFTER` (`200`) |
| A maze is cleared | `SCORE_CLEAR` (`500`) |

The score is one running total for the whole dive, carried across every maze of
it and reported as `score`. It rises by the exact figure above and by nothing
else. A dive opens at `0`, and losing a life leaves it as it stands.

## Lives and getting caught

A dive opens with `START_LIVES` (`3`) lives, reported as `lives`. That count is
the lives held in reserve: the life being played is not one of them.

Contact with a predator, defined in `specs/gameplay.md`, costs exactly one
life. While a life remains in reserve, `lives` falls by `1`, the maze is set up
for another attempt, and `screen` becomes `"countdown"`, so play resumes through
the dive countdown. The attempt starts from this arrangement:

- The forager is at rest on its start tile, with its brightness at `0`.
- Every predator is back in the den with its `released` flag `false`, and the
  staggered release schedule in `specs/predators.md` runs again from the moment
  live play resumes.
- Every bonus drifter is gone from the maze, and the drifter cadence restarts.
- Every sonar wavefront and every ink cloud is gone, and both cooldowns are
  ready.
- The plankton already eaten stay eaten, so `planktonRemaining` is unchanged.
- The score and the depth are unchanged.
- What the dive has revealed of the maze carries across, as `specs/sensing.md`
  gives.

Contact with no life in reserve ends the dive instead: `lives` is already `0`,
and `screen` becomes `"gameover"`.

## Descending to the next maze

A maze is cleared when the forager eats a plankton and none remain in the maze
after it. Clearing awards the `SCORE_CLEAR` bonus and `screen` becomes
`"cleared"`, and the next maze then begins at depth `d + 1`, opening on the dive
countdown as the first maze of the dive does. `specs/ui.md` fixes how long the
cleared screen holds.

The next maze starts fresh: plankton on every corridor tile outside the den, the
fog fully unrevealed, the forager at rest on its start tile, every predator in
the den with its `released` flag `false` and the staggered release schedule
running again from the moment live play begins, no bonus drifters, and the
drifter cadence restarted. The score and the lives in reserve carry across.

The layout of the next maze is a free choice. Reusing the current layout at every
depth and laying out a fresh one per depth both satisfy `specs/maze.md`.

## What depth scales

The depth `d` is a whole number from `1`, reported as `depth`, and the first maze
of a dive is depth `1`. Two things scale with it.

Deeper mazes hold more hunters. Depth `1` holds one predator of each kind, each
depth beyond the first adds one more, and the roster caps at two of each kind,
six predators in all, from depth `4` on. The kinds, the order they are added in,
and the release schedule are in `specs/predators.md`.

The sonar pulse's path range `E` shrinks by one tile per depth, down to a floor
of `SONAR_RANGE_MIN` (`5`) tiles from a base of `SONAR_RANGE_BASE` (`9`) tiles
at depth `1`:

`E = max(SONAR_RANGE_MIN, SONAR_RANGE_BASE - (d - 1))` tiles

| Depth `d` | `1` | `2` | `3` | `4` | `5` and deeper |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `E` | `9` | `8` | `7` | `6` | `5` |

`E` is reported as `sonar.range`, and it is the range every pulse the forager
emits carries, reported as that pulse's `range`.

Nothing else scales with depth. Every predator speed in `specs/predators.md` and
the files under `specs/predators/` holds at every depth, and so do the predators'
detection ranges and senses, brightness, the sonar and ink cooldowns, ink's
radius and life, the drifter cadence, and the scoring figures above.

## Audio

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The audio layer is part of the runtime you write. Every cue is synthesized with
the Web Audio API, and the build ships no audio files. Define and play exactly
the seven cues in `CUES`, under exactly these names, one per event:
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Audio plays through the engine's cue bus. Every cue is synthesized from its
declaration, and the build ships no audio files. Define and play exactly the
seven cues in `CUES`, under exactly these names, one per event:
{{else}}
Audio plays through the engine's cue bus: define exactly the seven cues in
`CUES` from the game instance's `initialize` and play them from the world's
audio inside a tick, under exactly these names, one per event:
{{/if}}

| Cue | Constant | Played when |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `eat` | `CUES.eat` | The forager eats a plankton. |
| `sonar` | `CUES.sonar` | The forager emits a sonar pulse. |
| `ink` | `CUES.ink` | The forager releases an ink cloud. |
| `predator-ping` | `CUES.predatorPing` | A Gloamfin emits one of its own sonar pings. |
| `flare` | `CUES.flare` | A Flarefish's bloom begins. |
| `caught` | `CUES.caught` | A predator makes contact with the forager. |
| `descend` | `CUES.descend` | A maze is cleared. |

Each is a distinct short sound, so the seven events are told apart by ear. Each
is played on the tick its event happens, and at most once on that tick: a tick
that raises more than one of them plays each of those once, and a tick on which
two creatures raise the same one plays it once.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Muting and the first-interaction unlock belong to the runtime you write. The mute
control `specs/movement.md` fixes toggles the runtime's mute bit from any screen,
and the game mirrors that bit into `muted` every frame. Sound starts only once
the player has interacted with the page, and a cue played before that is accepted
and simply sounds nothing.
{{else}}
Muting and the first-gesture unlock belong to the engine. The game binds the mute
control `specs/movement.md` fixes to the engine's mute bit and toggles it from
any screen, then mirrors that bit into `muted` every frame. A cue played before
the unlock is accepted and simply sounds nothing.
{{/if}}

While `muted` is on, no cue sounds. The events that raise cues still happen and
the game plays exactly as it does unmuted, and only the sound stops. Clearing
the toggle restores the cues from the next event on. A session opens with sound
on, so `muted` is `false` until the mute control is used.

The game stays fully playable with sound muted, and it runs, plays and reaches
every screen whether or not audio is available.

## Out of scope

- Network or online play.
- Predators that can be eaten, and power-ups that turn them into prey.
- Persistence of the score or of any setting between sessions. Each session
  starts fresh.
